WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.536
Picture a shop with two tills, and a list of cards to refuse, such as cards reported stolen.

2
00:00:08.536 --> 00:00:13.103
Each till keeps its own copy, and the copies sync over the shop's Wi-Fi.

3
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:15.369
One morning the Wi-Fi drops.

4
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:22.712
At the first till, a customer's lost card has turned up, so the cashier takes card 4417 off the list.

5
00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:29.160
At the second till, the bank phones about a stolen card, and the cashier adds card 5310.

6
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:32.431
When the Wi-Fi comes back, the two lists disagree.

7
00:00:32.431 --> 00:00:34.289
Which one should the shop keep?

8
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:37.237
The common answer is: keep the later one.

9
00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:40.818
On one computer, the later write is the newer decision.

10
00:00:40.818 --> 00:00:43.163
This rule is called last write wins.

11
00:00:43.433 --> 00:00:46.202
The first till's list says 10:00:05.

12
00:00:46.202 --> 00:00:48.125
The second says 10:00:03.

13
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:53.971
So the first list wins, the whole of it, and the second list is thrown away.

14
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:56.373
And with it, the stolen card.

15
00:00:56.373 --> 00:00:57.996
Card 5310 works again.

16
00:00:57.996 --> 00:00:59.545
No error, no warning.

17
00:00:59.545 --> 00:01:00.062
Well...

18
00:01:00.062 --> 00:01:03.824
the thief is having a better morning than the shop.

19
00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:05.611
Notice what went wrong.

20
00:01:05.611 --> 00:01:08.301
Neither cashier knew about the other's change.

21
00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:12.218
Later on the clock doesn't mean the writer had seen the other list.

22
00:01:12.218 --> 00:01:16.603
And if the first till's clock ran a few seconds fast, it wasn't even later.

23
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.013
What we want is for each list to carry what its writer had already seen.

24
00:01:21.013 --> 00:01:25.657
Then the shop could tell a list that replaces another from two lists that clash.

25
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:29.095
Here's an idea from everyday reading: bookmarks.

26
00:01:29.095 --> 00:01:32.348
Each till numbers its own changes, one, two, three.

27
00:01:32.348 --> 00:01:39.365
And every copy of the list carries one bookmark per till, saying how far into that till's changes it has read.

28
00:01:39.633 --> 00:01:45.457
In the app, the two tills are two copies of the list in two regions, Europe and the US.

29
00:01:45.457 --> 00:01:51.615
Before the Wi-Fi dropped, both copies had read EU's first two changes, and none from the US.

30
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:57.623
When a copy takes a change, it moves its own bookmark forward by one.

31
00:01:57.623 --> 00:02:02.517
EU removes card 4417, so its EU bookmark goes from 2 to 3.

32
00:02:02.517 --> 00:02:07.159
US adds card 5310, so its US bookmark goes from 0 to 1.

33
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.346
Now the test.

34
00:02:08.346 --> 00:02:10.166
Take the easy case first.

35
00:02:10.166 --> 00:02:13.804
EU's list before the change had bookmarks 2 and 0.

36
00:02:13.804 --> 00:02:14.896
After, 3 and 0.

37
00:02:14.896 --> 00:02:21.810
Every bookmark is at least as far along, so the newer list has read everything the old one had.

38
00:02:21.810 --> 00:02:23.120
It can replace it.

39
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:24.513
Now the clash.

40
00:02:24.513 --> 00:02:26.534
EU's bookmarks: 3 and 0.

41
00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:27.714
US's: 2 and 1.

42
00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.915
Did either one see the other's change?

43
00:02:30.915 --> 00:02:32.263
Pause and guess.

44
00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:37.489
Compare them one at a time.

45
00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:40.686
On EU's changes, EU is ahead, 3 against 2.

46
00:02:40.686 --> 00:02:43.883
On US's changes, US is ahead, 1 against 0.

47
00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:47.004
Each has read something the other hasn't.

48
00:02:47.004 --> 00:02:48.907
So neither saw the other.

49
00:02:49.067 --> 00:02:50.039
That's the test.

50
00:02:50.039 --> 00:02:55.693
If every entry of one is at most the other's, the second saw the first, and the first can go.

51
00:02:55.693 --> 00:03:00.799
If each is ahead somewhere, the writes are concurrent: neither knew about the other.

52
00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:05.380
The bookmarks have a name: a version vector, one counter per copy.

53
00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:10.583
Dynamo, Amazon's design for a store with many copies, called it a vector clock.

54
00:03:10.833 --> 00:03:14.807
And when two versions are concurrent, Dynamo doesn't pick one.

55
00:03:14.807 --> 00:03:20.511
It keeps both, side by side, as siblings, and hands both to the program on the next read.

56
00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.222
So the program has to merge them.

57
00:03:23.222 --> 00:03:27.404
The obvious merge: keep every card that's on either list.

58
00:03:27.404 --> 00:03:29.753
That keeps the stolen card 5310.

59
00:03:29.753 --> 00:03:30.853
So far so good.

60
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:31.785
But look.

61
00:03:31.785 --> 00:03:33.156
Card 4417 is back.

62
00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:40.697
EU removed it, but the US list still had it, and a missing card could mean removed, or never added.

63
00:03:40.697 --> 00:03:42.753
The union can't tell which.

64
00:03:42.967 --> 00:03:46.970
So the customer whose lost card turned up gets refused again.

65
00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.201
Version vectors noticed the clash.

66
00:03:49.201 --> 00:03:50.645
They didn't settle it.

67
00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.083
Two more limits.

68
00:03:52.083 --> 00:04:01.019
Your code gets the siblings and has to merge them well, and the Dynamo paper warns about exactly this: a deleted item can come back.

69
00:04:01.267 --> 00:04:04.732
And the vector grows with every copy that takes writes.

70
00:04:04.732 --> 00:04:07.504
Dynamo cut its vectors at about ten entries.

71
00:04:07.504 --> 00:04:12.103
Next, we'll meet data types that merge themselves, with no code from you.

72
00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:14.709
So, which list should the shop keep?

73
00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:15.847
Not the later one.

74
00:04:15.847 --> 00:04:18.376
Keep a version only if it saw the other.

75
00:04:18.376 --> 00:04:20.652
If neither saw the other, keep both.

76
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.179
One quick check.

77
00:04:22.179 --> 00:04:28.180
The program merges the siblings and writes the result through EU, with bookmarks 4 and 1.

78
00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:30.203
Does it replace both siblings?

79
00:04:30.203 --> 00:04:31.281
Pause and think.

80
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:34.810
Yes.

81
00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:38.690
Against EU's 3 and 0, it's ahead or equal on both.

82
00:04:38.690 --> 00:04:41.095
Against US's 2 and 1, the same.

83
00:04:41.095 --> 00:04:44.276
It has read everything both siblings had.
